            <ArticleTitle>Enhanced Recovery Pathways in Gynecology and Gynecologic Oncology.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>780-92</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/OGX.0000000000000259</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="IMPORTANCE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Enhanced recovery programs (ERPs) are considered standard of care across a variety of surgical disciplines, but ERPs have not been widely adopted in gynecology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to describe ERP principles and the role of ERPs in gynecology and gynecologic oncology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="EVIDENCE ACQUISITION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Comprehensive literature search was performed using MEDLINE, the Cochrane Collaboration Database, and PubMed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Meta-analyses of a substantial number of randomized controlled trials have shown that implementation of ERP protocols is associated with decreased length of hospital stay, a decrease in rates of postoperative complication, decreased morbidity, and cost savings while preserving patient satisfaction and quality of life.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS AND RELEVANCE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">High-quality evidence exists for improved outcomes among patients in ERPs. Enhanced recovery programs save resources and costs across the health care system. As quality metrics and bundled payments increase in health care, ERPs will have increasing prominence.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
